WN.lb(48,{"mt_-gkJdxJUQT":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the pupil?","choices":["A hole that lets light in","A black dot painted on the eye","The coloured part of the eye"],"answer":0,"hint":"Light has to get inside the eye somehow. Where does it go in?","explain":"The pupil is a hole in the iris. It looks black because light goes in and does not come back out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You walk into a dark cave. What do your pupils do?","choices":["Get bigger","Get smaller","Stay the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"There is not much light in a cave. Does the eye need more or less light to get in?","explain":"In the dark the pupils get bigger, so more light can get into the eye."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Tom says his eyes shine a beam onto the things he looks at. Is he right?","choices":["No, light bounces off things into his eyes","Yes, that is how eyes work","Only in the dark"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see anything in a room with no light at all?","explain":"In a room with no light you see nothing, so eyes cannot make their own light. Light from a source bounces off things into our eyes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A bike light is {a} m from a sign. Light bounces off the sign and goes {b} m to your eye. How far does the light travel in all, in metres?","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,8]},"hint":"Add the trip from the bike light to the sign and the trip from the sign to your eye.","explain":"{a} m to the sign and {b} m back to your eye: {a} + {b} = {=a+b} m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What does the lens do?","choices":["Bends the light so the picture is sharp","Makes the eye a colour","Sends the message to the brain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a magnifying glass. What does it do to light?","explain":"The lens bends the light so it meets on the retina and the picture is sharp. The iris gives the eye its colour, and the optic nerve carries the message."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which part works out what you are looking at?","choices":["The brain","The lens","The pupil"],"answer":0,"hint":"The eye catches the light. Where does the message go?","explain":"The eye collects the light, and the optic nerve takes the message to the brain. The brain works out what you see."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Your pupils are {a} mm wide in bright sun. In a dim room they get {b} mm wider. How wide are they now, in mm?","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[2,3],"b":[2,4]},"hint":"In dim light the pupil opens up, so add the change.","explain":"Dim light makes the pupils open up: {a} + {b} = {=a+b} mm, so more light gets in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Put these in order: brain, pupil, retina, lens, optic nerve. Which comes third?","choices":["Retina","Lens","Optic nerve"],"answer":0,"hint":"Light goes in through the pupil, then the lens bends it. Where does it land?","explain":"Pupil, lens, retina, optic nerve, brain. The retina is third: the picture lands there after the lens bends the light."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which part is the coloured ring at the front of the eye?","choices":["The iris","The pupil","The retina"],"answer":0,"hint":"Blue, brown or green eyes: which part has the colour?","explain":"The iris is the coloured ring. It changes the size of the pupil in the middle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Why must you never look straight at the Sun?","choices":["Its strong light can harm the retina","It makes the pupil too big","The lens will fall out"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part at the back of the eye catches the light?","explain":"The Sun's light is so strong that it can burn the retina, even when the pupil is small. Sunglasses do not make it safe."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You look at a teddy in a lit room. Which way does the light go?","choices":["From the lamp, off the teddy, into my eyes","From my eyes out to the teddy","From the teddy, which glows by itself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which thing makes light? Follow the light from there.","explain":"The lamp makes the light. It bounces off the teddy and goes into your eyes. Eyes take light in; they do not send it out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Light goes from a lamp to a toy, then into your eye. The lamp is {a} m from the toy. The toy is {b} m from your eye. How many metres does the light travel?","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"The light goes lamp to toy, then toy to eye. Add the two parts of the trip.","explain":"The light travels {a} m to the toy and then {b} m to your eye: {a} + {b} = {=a+b} m."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a dark room your pupil is {a} mm wide. You step into bright sun and it gets {b} mm smaller. How wide is it now, in mm?","answer":"a-b","params":{"a":[6,8],"b":[2,4]},"hint":"Bright light makes the pupil smaller. Take the change away from the dark-room size.","explain":"The pupil shrinks in bright light: {a} − {b} = {=a-b} mm. A smaller pupil lets less light in."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Where does the sharp picture land inside the eye?","choices":["On the retina at the back","On the lens","On the pupil"],"answer":0,"hint":"The lens bends the light. Where does the bent light end up?","explain":"The lens bends the light so it meets on the retina at the back of the eye. That is where the picture forms."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which part carries the message from the eye to the brain?","choices":["The optic nerve","The iris","The lens"],"answer":0,"hint":"It leaves the back of the eye like a cable.","explain":"The optic nerve carries the message from the retina to the brain. The iris and lens are at the front and work on the light."}],"explain":{"kid":["You see a ball when light from a lamp hits it and bounces into your eyes. Your eyes take light in. They do not send light out.","Light goes in through the pupil. The pupil is a hole, not a dot. It looks black because light goes in and does not come back out. The coloured ring round it is the iris. In the dark, your pupil gets big to let more light in. In bright sun, it gets small. The iris does this by itself.","Behind the pupil is the lens. It bends the light so a sharp picture lands on the retina at the back. The picture there is upside down!","The retina sends a message along the optic nerve to your brain. Your brain works out what you see. It turns the picture the right way up."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. England's Year 6 science asks children to explain that we see things because light travels from light sources to our eyes, or from light sources to objects and then to our eyes. The parts of the eye (pupil, iris, lens, retina, optic nerve) are named in BBC Bitesize KS2 'How do we see?' and taught in full at KS3. Key words: light source, reflect, pupil, iris, lens, retina, optic nerve.","Make the path of light visible. In a dark room, shine a torch on a toy and ask where the light goes next. Draw arrows from the torch to the toy and from the toy to the eye, never from the eye outwards. Then look at each other's pupils in dim light and in bright light.","Common mistakes: thinking eyes send out rays to the things we look at (a well-documented idea in children, Driver and others, 1985); calling the pupil a black dot rather than a hole; expecting the pupil to shrink in the dark; thinking the picture forms on the lens or the pupil; and thinking the eye, not the brain, makes sense of what we see. Be careful with 'the eye sees': the eye collects light, the brain interprets it.","In the lab your child taps where light goes to see a ball and then a lamp, drags names onto a side view of the eye, picks the pupil size for a sunny beach, a dark bedroom and a classroom, makes the lens thinner for a far tree and fatter for a near book until the picture is sharp (and upside down) on the retina, and orders the five steps from pupil to brain."]},"familyTip":"Stand by a mirror in a dim room and look at your child's pupils. Switch on a bright light and watch them shrink, then switch it off and count how long they take to open up again. Ask: why does your eye do that? Never look straight at the Sun, even with sunglasses.","keyIdea":"We see when light gets into our eyes. Light from a lamp or the Sun bounces off things and goes in through the pupil, a hole in the coloured iris. The lens bends the light so a sharp picture lands on the retina at the back of the eye. The retina sends a message along the optic nerve, and the brain works out what we are looking at. The iris makes the pupil small in bright light and big in the dark.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Front or back of the eye? Drag each part, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Front of the eye","Back of the eye"],"items":[["Pupil",0],["Retina",1],["Iris",0],["Optic nerve",1],["Lens",0],["Where the picture lands",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flageye","caption":"Flagship lab: send lamp light off a ball into an eye, label the eye, set the pupil, focus the lens on the retina and order the steps to the brain.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_C7FNeIDGc6":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Can you see one of your skin cells with just your eyes?","choices":["No, you need a microscope","Yes, if you look closely","Yes, it is as big as a freckle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scientists use a special tool to look at cells. Why?","explain":"Most cells are far too small to see with your eyes. A microscope makes them look big enough to see."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Muscle tissue, lining tissue and nerve tissue work together in the stomach. So the stomach is…","choices":["An organ","A tissue","A cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we call several different tissues working together?","explain":"Different tissues working together make an organ. The stomach is an organ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia says a tissue is bigger than an organ. Is she right?","choices":["No, an organ is made of tissues, so it is bigger","Yes, tissues are the biggest","They are the same size"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is made of the other?","explain":"An organ is built from several tissues, so the organ is bigger than any one of its tissues."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An organ system has {a} organs. Scientists add {b} more organs that work in the same system. How many organs are in the system now?","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[1,3]},"hint":"Add the new organs to the ones already in the system.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} organs, all working together in one organ system."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which system are the heart and blood vessels part of?","choices":["The circulatory system","The digestive system","The breathing system"],"answer":0,"hint":"The heart pumps blood. Which system moves blood round the body?","explain":"The heart pumps blood through the blood vessels. Together they are the circulatory system."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam puts a nerve cell into his muscle tissue model. What is wrong?","choices":["A tissue is made of similar cells, so muscle tissue needs muscle cells","Nothing, any cell will do","Tissues have no cells"],"answer":0,"hint":"What kind of cells does muscle tissue need?","explain":"Muscle tissue is a group of similar cells, muscle cells, that all squeeze. A nerve cell belongs in nerve tissue."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A model has {a} rows of {b} cells. How many cells is that?","answer":"a*b","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,5]},"hint":"Multiply the number of rows by the cells in each row.","explain":"{a} rows of {b} cells: {a} × {b} = {=a*b} cells in the model tissue."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is the biggest level?","choices":["The whole body","An organ system","An organ"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do all the systems make together?","explain":"All the organ systems work together to make the whole body, the biggest level."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which pair are both in the breathing system?","choices":["Lungs and windpipe","Heart and lungs","Stomach and windpipe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which organs carry and take in air?","explain":"The windpipe carries air to the lungs, so both are in the breathing system. The heart is in the circulatory system."},{"type":"mc","id":"m10","prompt":"Why does the stomach need muscle tissue?","choices":["To squeeze and churn the food","To think","To hold the body up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Muscle tissue is good at one job. What is it?","explain":"Muscle tissue squeezes. In the stomach it churns food and mixes it with juices."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is a tissue?","choices":["A group of similar cells doing one job","Any mix of cells","One very big cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of muscle tissue. What kind of cells is it made of?","explain":"A tissue is a group of similar cells working together on one job, like muscle cells making muscle tissue."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An organ is made of {a} kinds of tissue. You add {b} more kinds of tissue to your model. How many kinds of tissue does the model have now?","answer":"a+b","params":{"a":[2,3],"b":[1,2]},"hint":"Organs are made of several tissues. Add the new kinds to the ones you had.","explain":"{a} kinds plus {b} more kinds makes {=a+b} kinds of tissue, all working together in one organ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A tissue model needs {a} cells in each row and has {b} rows. How many cells are in the tissue?","answer":"a*b","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[2,4]},"hint":"Count the cells in one row, then count the rows. Multiply.","explain":"{a} cells in each of {b} rows: {a} × {b} = {=a*b} cells. A real tissue has millions of cells."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which list goes from smallest to biggest?","choices":["Cell, tissue, organ, organ system","Tissue, cell, organ, organ system","Organ, tissue, cell, organ system"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is a tissue made of? Start with that.","explain":"Cells make tissues, tissues make organs and organs make organ systems, so the order is cell, tissue, organ, organ system."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which system is the stomach part of?","choices":["The digestive system","The circulatory system","The breathing system"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the stomach do with your food?","explain":"The stomach breaks down food, so it is part of the digestive system, with the intestines."}],"explain":{"kid":["Your body is made of cells. Cells are tiny. Most are far too small to see with just your eyes. You need a microscope to see them.","Lots of similar cells working together make a tissue. Muscle cells make muscle tissue, which can squeeze. Nerve cells make nerve tissue, which carries messages.","Different tissues work together to make an organ. Your stomach has muscle tissue to churn food, lining tissue to make juices, and nerve tissue to send messages. So an organ is bigger than a tissue.","Organs work in teams called organ systems. The heart and blood vessels make the circulatory system. The stomach and intestines are in the digestive system. All the systems together make you!"],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. Living things are built in levels: cells, tissues, organs, organ systems and the whole organism. England's KS3 biology names this as 'the hierarchical organisation of multicellular organisms'. Many primary schemes and BBC Bitesize start it earlier. Key words: cell, tissue, organ, organ system, organism, microscope.","Make the levels with your hands. One Lego brick is a cell. A row of matching bricks is a tissue. A few different rows clipped together make an organ, and a set of those is a system. Ask your child to point to each level and say what it is made of.","Common mistakes: thinking cells are big enough to see; thinking a tissue is any mix of cells, not a group of similar cells with one job; thinking an organ has only one tissue; mixing up which is bigger, tissue or organ; and putting an organ in the wrong system, such as the heart in the breathing system because it sits by the lungs. Be careful to say 'similar cells', since real tissues also hold a few helper cells.","In the lab your child zooms from the body to the circulatory system, the heart, heart muscle and one muscle cell. They build muscle and then nerve tissue from a tray of mixed cells, and build the stomach from muscle, lining and nerve tissue (bone does not fit). Then they sort six organs into three systems and put the five levels in order of size."]},"familyTip":"Build the levels with Lego or pasta: one brick is a cell, a row of the same bricks is a tissue, a few different rows stuck together are an organ, and a set of organs is a system. Then name an organ at dinner (stomach, heart, lungs) and ask which system it belongs to.","keyIdea":"Your body is built in levels. Cells are the tiny building blocks, too small to see without a microscope. A group of similar cells doing one job is a tissue, like muscle tissue. Different tissues working together make an organ, like the stomach or the heart. Organs work together in an organ system, like the circulatory system, and all the systems together make your body.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which level is it? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Cell or tissue","Organ or organ system"],"items":[["One muscle cell",0],["The heart",1],["Muscle tissue",0],["The digestive system",1],["A nerve cell",0],["The lungs",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtissue","caption":"Flagship lab: zoom from the body to one cell, build tissues from similar cells, build the stomach from tissues, sort organs into systems and order the levels.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4JpMXUIxeD":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the job of skin in fighting germs?","choices":["It is a wall that keeps germs out","It makes antibodies","It swallows germs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Skin is on the outside of your whole body.","explain":"Unbroken skin is a tough barrier that germs cannot get through."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why should a cut be cleaned and covered?","choices":["Broken skin lets germs in","Plasters kill all germs","Cuts make antibodies"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has happened to the skin wall?","explain":"A cut is a gap in the skin wall, so germs can get in. A plaster covers the gap until new skin grows."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A germ splits in two every 20 minutes. You start with {n} germs. How many are there after one hour?","answer":"n*8","params":{"n":[1,5]},"hint":"One hour is three lots of 20 minutes. Double three times.","explain":"{n} → {=n*2} → {=n*4} → {=n*8}, so there are {=n*8} germs after one hour."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What do some white blood cells do to germs?","choices":["Swallow them and break them down","Turn them into antibodies","Push them out through the skin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the white blood cell in the lab moving onto each germ.","explain":"Phagocytes are white blood cells that swallow germs and break them down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You had chickenpox. Years later the same germ gets in. What usually happens?","choices":["Memory cells act fast, so you stay well","You get just as ill again","You get even more ill"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do memory cells remember?","explain":"Memory cells remember the germ, so lots of antibodies come within days and you usually stay well."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"In a class of {n} children, {k} have had a vaccine for a germ. How many children have not had it?","answer":"n-k","params":{"n":[25,32],"k":[18,24]},"hint":"Take the vaccinated children away from the whole class.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} children have not had the vaccine, so their bodies have not learned that germ yet."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A cold is caused by a virus. Will antibiotics cure it?","choices":["No, antibiotics work on bacteria, not viruses","Yes, antibiotics kill all germs","Yes, if you take extra"],"answer":0,"hint":"Antibiotics only work on one kind of germ.","explain":"Antibiotics kill bacteria. Colds are caused by viruses, so antibiotics do not help; rest and drinks do."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is inside a vaccine?","choices":["A weak, dead or part of a germ","A strong live germ","Antibiotics"],"answer":0,"hint":"It must be safe, but still look like the germ.","explain":"A vaccine holds a weak, dead or part of a germ, so the body can learn it without getting the illness."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which part of the body traps germs you breathe in?","choices":["Sticky mucus in the nose","Acid in the stomach","Tears in the eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the air goes in when you breathe.","explain":"Air goes in through the nose, where sticky mucus and tiny hairs trap germs."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A germ splits in two every 20 minutes. {n} germs get into a cut. How many germs are there after 40 minutes if nothing stops them?","answer":"n*4","params":{"n":[2,6]},"hint":"40 minutes is two lots of 20 minutes, so the number doubles twice.","explain":"{n} doubles to {=n*2} after 20 minutes, then to {=n*4} after 40 minutes."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Your body has antibodies for chickenpox. A cold germ gets in. What happens?","choices":["Your body must make new antibodies for the cold germ","The chickenpox antibodies stop it","Antibodies stop any germ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does one key open every lock?","explain":"Each antibody fits only one kind of germ, so the body has to make new antibodies for the cold germ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The first time a germ gets in, the body takes about {a} days to make enough antibodies. The second time it takes {b} days. How many days faster is the second time?","answer":"a-b","params":{"a":[10,14],"b":[2,4]},"hint":"Take the second time away from the first time.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} days faster, because memory cells remember the germ."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why does a vaccine protect you?","choices":["It lets your body learn a germ safely","It is a medicine that kills germs","It gives you the full illness"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is inside a vaccine, and what does the body do with it?","explain":"A vaccine holds a weak, dead or part of a germ. The body makes memory cells without the illness, so it is ready for the real germ."}],"explain":{"kid":["Your body has more than one way to stop you getting ill. First it keeps germs out. Skin is a wall, sticky mucus in the nose traps germs you breathe in, tears wash the eyes, and acid in the stomach kills germs you swallow.","A few germs can still get in, for example through a cut. Some split in two about every 20 minutes, so two can become eight in 40 minutes. White blood cells find them and swallow them.","Other white blood cells make antibodies. Each antibody fits only one kind of germ, like a key fits one lock. The first time, making enough takes days, and you may feel ill. Memory cells stay, so the next time is fast and you usually stay well.","A vaccine lets your body meet a weak, dead or part of a germ, so you do not get the illness. Your body still makes memory cells. Then, if the real germ comes, you are ready. Antibiotics are different: they kill bacteria, but they do not work on viruses like colds."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. The first line of defence is barriers: intact skin, mucus and cilia in the airways, tears containing the enzyme lysozyme, and stomach acid. Pathogens that get past them can multiply quickly; some bacteria divide about every 20 minutes in good conditions. White blood cells called phagocytes engulf and digest them.","The second line is slower the first time. Lymphocytes make antibodies, and each antibody has a shape that binds one antigen on one kind of pathogen. Afterwards memory cells remain, so a later infection by the same pathogen produces a faster, larger antibody response, often before any symptoms. This is why most people get chickenpox only once.","A common mistake is to think a vaccine is a medicine that kills germs, or that it gives you the full disease. A vaccine contains a weakened, inactivated or partial form of a pathogen, or the instructions for one part, so the body builds memory cells safely. Be careful with another slip: antibiotics act on bacteria, not viruses, so they do not cure colds or flu.","In the lab your child guards four ways in and covers a cut, watches two germs become eight and has a white blood cell swallow them, fits antibodies to the matching germs, meets the same germ twice on an antibody graph, and vaccinates Ava but not Ben, then compares who stays well."]},"familyTip":"Next time someone has a cut, a cold or a jab, talk it through together. Why does a plaster help? Why does a sneeze go in a tissue? Look at a vaccination record if you have one and ask: which germs has your body already learned, and why do you not get chickenpox twice?","keyIdea":"Your immune system protects you from germs (bacteria and viruses) in layers. First, barriers keep most germs out: skin, sticky mucus in the nose, tears and stomach acid. Germs that get in can multiply fast, so white blood cells swallow them. Other white blood cells make antibodies, and each antibody fits only one kind of germ. The first time, this takes days and you may feel ill. Memory cells then remember the germ, so the next time the body fights it fast. A vaccine lets your body learn a germ safely, without the illness. Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which defence is it: keeping germs out, or fighting germs inside? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Keeps germs out","Fights germs inside"],"items":[["Skin covers the body",0],["Mucus in the nose traps germs",0],["Stomach acid kills germs in food",0],["A white blood cell swallows a germ",1],["Antibodies stick to one kind of germ",1],["Memory cells remember a germ",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagimmune","caption":"Flagship lab: guard four ways in, let germs double and swallow them, fit antibodies to germs, meet a germ twice, then vaccinate Ava.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4k3HGahK2k":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Each time you have something sugary, your teeth are under acid attack for up to an hour. You have sugary snacks at {a} different times today. Up to how many hours of acid attack is that?","answer":"a","params":{"a":[2,6]},"hint":"Count one attack of up to an hour for each sugar time.","explain":"{a} sugar times means up to {a} hours of acid attack. Fewer sugar times means fewer attacks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A resting heart beats {a} times a minute. How many times does it beat in {b} minutes?","answer":"a*b","params":{"a":[70,90],"b":[2,5]},"hint":"Multiply the beats in one minute by the number of minutes.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b} beats in {b} minutes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Someone is active every day for months. What happens to their resting heart rate?","choices":["It goes down a little","It goes up, because the heart is worn out","It does not change"],"answer":0,"hint":"A stronger heart pumps more blood with each beat.","explain":"Exercise strengthens the heart, so each beat pumps more blood and fewer beats are needed at rest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which of these counts towards your 60 active minutes?","choices":["A 20-minute bike ride","An hour of an exciting video game","Watching a football match on TV"],"answer":0,"hint":"Active time makes you warm and out of breath.","explain":"The bike ride makes you puff, so it counts. Screen time is sitting time, however exciting it is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which has free sugar that counts towards the 24 g limit?","choices":["A glass of orange juice","A whole orange","A glass of plain milk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Juicing breaks open the fruit and frees its sugar.","explain":"Juice sugar is free sugar. The sugar inside a whole orange and in plain milk is not."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A 330 ml can of cola has about 35 g of sugar. For a 9-year-old, that is…","choices":["more than the whole day's 24 g","about a quarter of the day's limit","fine, because it is a drink"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare 35 g with the 24 g limit for ages 7 to 10.","explain":"35 g is more than 24 g, so one can goes over the whole day's limit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Smoking harms…","choices":["the lungs, the heart and the blood","only the lungs","only people who smoke a lot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about tar in the lungs and carbon monoxide in the blood.","explain":"Tar clogs the lungs, carbon monoxide takes oxygen's place in the blood, and the heart has to work harder."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"About how much sleep does a 10-year-old need each night?","choices":["9 to 12 hours","5 to 6 hours","14 to 16 hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"Children aged 6 to 12 need more sleep than adults.","explain":"Sleep experts advise 9 to 12 hours a night for children aged 6 to 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What do the UK's top doctors say is the healthiest choice about alcohol for children?","choices":["No alcohol at all","A little at parties","Only beer, not wine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Alcohol harms the liver and a brain that is still growing.","explain":"The Chief Medical Officers advise an alcohol-free childhood, because alcohol harms the liver and the growing brain."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why does your heart beat faster when you run?","choices":["Your muscles need more oxygen, so more blood must reach them","Your heart is getting tired","Running squeezes the heart"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do working muscles need more of, and what carries it?","explain":"Working muscles use more oxygen. Blood carries it, so the heart beats faster to send more blood."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You walk to school for {a} minutes and play tag for {b} minutes. How many more active minutes do you need to reach 60?","answer":"60-a-b","params":{"a":[10,20],"b":[10,25]},"hint":"Add the two active times, then take the total away from 60.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} active minutes, so you need 60 − {=a+b} = {=60-a-b} more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"At ages 7 to 10 the limit is 24 g of free sugar a day. A child drinks a juice with {a} g. How many grams are left for the rest of the day?","answer":"24-a","params":{"a":[8,16]},"hint":"Juice sugar is free sugar. Take it away from 24.","explain":"24 − {a} = {=24-a} g of free sugar left for the day."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which plan gives your teeth the fewest acid attacks?","choices":["Eat the sweets all together after lunch","Eat one sweet every hour","Suck one sweet slowly all afternoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each time sugar goes in, acid attacks teeth for up to an hour.","explain":"All together after lunch is one sugar time, so one acid attack. Spreading them out makes many attacks."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What does carbon monoxide in cigarette smoke do?","choices":["It takes the place of oxygen in red blood cells","It cleans the lungs","It makes the blood carry more oxygen"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the seats on the red blood cells in the lab.","explain":"Carbon monoxide grabs the seats where oxygen should ride and holds on tightly, so less oxygen reaches the body."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some everyday choices help your body work well. Being active, eating well and sleeping enough all help. So does keeping away from smoke and alcohol. When you run, your muscles need more oxygen. So your heart beats faster and you breathe faster to bring it. When you rest, both slow down again.","Your heart is a muscle, so exercise makes it stronger. A strong heart pumps more blood with each beat. Then it needs fewer beats when you rest. You need at least 60 active minutes a day. Active means you get warm and puff, like on a bike ride. Short bursts add up. Sitting at a screen does not count.","Free sugar is sugar added to food and drinks. Honey, syrup and juice count too. The sugar inside a whole apple is not free sugar, and nor is the sugar in plain milk. At 7 to 10, have no more than 24 g a day. One can of cola has about 35 g. Each time you have sugar, germs on your teeth make acid for up to an hour. So sweets with a meal do less harm than sweets all day.","Smoke from cigarettes has a gas in it called carbon monoxide. It takes the place of oxygen in your blood and will not let go. Tar in smoke clogs up the lungs. Alcohol harms the liver and a growing brain, so the best choice for children is none. Sleep matters too. At your age you need about 9 to 12 hours a night."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. This is England's Year 6 science statement 'recognise the impact of diet, exercise, drugs and lifestyle on the way their bodies function'. It builds on Year 2 (exercise, eating the right amounts of different types of food, and hygiene) and on the Year 6 circulatory system. Key words: lifestyle, heart rate, resting heart rate, circulatory system, free sugars, tooth decay, carbon monoxide, tar, nicotine, addiction.","The UK Chief Medical Officers (2019) ask 5 to 18 year olds for an average of at least 60 minutes of moderate to vigorous activity a day across the week. The NHS talk test sorts it: moderate means you can talk but not sing, and vigorous means you can only say a few words. During exercise, heart rate and breathing rise to deliver more oxygen. Regular training strengthens the heart, so it pumps more blood per beat and the resting rate falls. The lab's heart rates are example values for a 10-year-old.","Free sugars are sugars added to food and drink, plus those in honey, syrups, juices and smoothies. Sugars inside whole fruit and vegetables and in plain milk are not free sugars. The NHS limits are 19 g a day at 4 to 6, 24 g at 7 to 10 and 30 g from 11, and juice and smoothies together at most 150 ml a day. A 330 ml can of cola has about 35 g. The Oral Health Foundation notes that each sugar intake starts an acid attack of up to an hour, so how often counts more than how much.","Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin over 200 times more tightly than oxygen; smokers' blood usually has 3 to 10% of its haemoglobin carrying it instead of oxygen, and heavy smokers up to 15% (RIVM; Blumenthal 2001). Tar clogs the airways, and nicotine is addictive. The Chief Medical Officers advise an alcohol-free childhood. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine (2016) advises 9 to 12 hours of sleep for ages 6 to 12. Common mistakes: thinking exercise wears the heart out; thinking only one long session counts; counting exciting screen games as active; thinking juice is fine because it is fruit; counting whole fruit as free sugar; thinking small sweets spread through the day are kinder to teeth; and thinking smoking only harms the lungs. In the lab the learner runs and rests, trains a heart for 8 weeks, builds 60 active minutes, picks 4 items under 24 g, times 4 sweets and breathes smoky air."]},"familyTip":"Find your pulse on the wrist together, count it for 15 seconds and times it by 4. Jog on the spot for a minute and count again. Then look at a can of fizzy drink at home and find how many grams of sugar it has.","keyIdea":"Everyday choices change how your body works. When you run, your muscles need more oxygen, so your heart beats faster and you breathe faster. Regular exercise makes the heart muscle stronger, so each beat pumps more blood and the resting heart rate falls. Children need at least 60 active minutes a day on average, and short bursts add up. Free sugar (added sugar, honey, syrup and juice) should stay under 24 g a day at ages 7 to 10, and sugar at fewer times means fewer acid attacks on teeth. Smoke puts carbon monoxide in the blood and tar in the lungs, alcohol harms the liver and a growing brain, and children need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Free sugar or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Free sugar","Not free sugar"],"items":[["Orange juice",0],["Whole apple",1],["Honey",0],["Plain milk",1],["Cola",0],["Banana",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglifechoice","caption":"Flagship lab: run and rest, train a heart, build 60 active minutes, keep sugar under 24 g, time sweets for teeth, and breathe smoky air.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_A1_5K47UNb":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Zoe sings Happy Birthday while she scrubs. One song takes 10 seconds. She sings it {n} times. How many seconds does she scrub?","answer":"n*10","hint":"Count in tens, {n} times.","explain":"{n} lots of 10 seconds is {=n*10} seconds. Twice through, 20 seconds, is enough.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Sam plays outside for {n} minutes. Then he comes in for lunch. What should he do?","choices":["Wash his hands before he eats","Eat first, wash later","Nothing, his hands look fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Playing outside can put germs on your hands. When should they come off?","explain":"He should wash his hands before he eats, so the germs from outside do not get onto his food.","params":{"n":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You need to sneeze. What is the best thing to do?","choices":["Sneeze into a tissue and bin it","Sneeze into your hands","Sneeze into the air"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one stops the germs from reaching other people and things?","explain":"Use a tissue, put it in the bin, then wash your hands. Sneezing into hands or the air spreads germs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which part of the hands do people often forget to wash?","choices":["Thumbs and nails","The palms","None, people wash every part"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the parts you scrubbed last in the lab.","explain":"Thumbs, nails, the backs and between the fingers are often missed. Germs hide there, so scrub every part."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How do germs get from one person to another?","choices":["On hands and things we touch, and in sneezes","Only by looking at someone","They cannot move at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, where did the germs go?","explain":"Germs spread on hands, on things we touch like toys and door handles, and in sneezes and coughs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is a time to wash your hands?","choices":["After using the toilet","Before watching TV","Before reading a book"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could put germs on your hands?","explain":"After using the toilet. Germs from the toilet can get on your hands, so wash them with soap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben says all germs are bad. Is he right?","choices":["No, most are harmless, but some make us ill","Yes, every germ makes you ill","Yes, germs are always big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do we get ill every time we touch something?","explain":"No. Most germs are harmless. Some can make us ill, so we wash our hands to get rid of them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ava rinses her hands under the tap for 1 second with no soap. Are they clean?","choices":["No, she needs soap and about 20 seconds","Yes, water is enough","Yes, 1 second is plenty"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, what did water on its own do?","explain":"No. A quick rinse leaves lots of germs. Soap and about 20 seconds of scrubbing get them off."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Leo has scrubbed his hands with soap for {a} seconds. He needs to scrub for 20 seconds. How many more seconds does he need?","answer":"20-a","hint":"Start at {a} and count on in ones until you reach 20. The number of counts is the answer.","explain":"20 − {a} = {=20-a}, so Leo needs {=20-a} more seconds of scrubbing.","params":{"a":[5,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia strokes her dog {n} times. Then it is time for her snack. What should she do first?","choices":["Wash her hands with soap","Eat her snack straight away","Wipe her hands on her top"],"answer":0,"hint":"Germs from the dog could get on her food. How does she get them off?","explain":"She should wash with soap first. Germs from the dog are on her hands, and they could get onto her food.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which washes the most germs off your hands?","choices":["Soap and water","Water only","A quick shake"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, which one got the hand all clean?","explain":"Soap and water. Water alone leaves lots of germs behind. Soap lifts them off so the water can rinse them away."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why should you wash your hands even when they look clean?","choices":["Germs are too small to see","To make them look shiny","Soap is fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you see germs with just your eyes?","explain":"Germs are far too small to see. Hands can look clean and still have germs on them."}],"explain":{"kid":["Germs are tiny living things. They are so small you cannot see them. Most do no harm, but some can make you ill, like when you get a cold or a tummy bug.","Germs get on your hands. Then they go on all the things you touch, like toys, door handles and food. If they get in your mouth, they can make you ill.","Water on its own gets a few germs off. Soap gets lots more off. Rub your palms, the backs, between your fingers, your thumbs and your nails. Sing Happy Birthday twice. Then rinse and dry.","Wash your hands before you eat. Wash them after the toilet, after you stroke a pet and after you play outside. When you sneeze, use a tissue. Put it in the bin, then wash your hands."],"adult":["In England, Year 2 science asks children to describe the importance for humans of exercise, eating the right amounts of different types of food, and hygiene. This lesson covers the hygiene part. It links to the health education guidance, which says children should know about germs, how they spread and the importance of handwashing.","The key words are germs, hygiene, spread and soap. Germs is the everyday word for microbes such as bacteria and viruses. Most are harmless, but some cause illness. Soap works because it lifts the oils and dirt that germs cling to, so the water can rinse them away. The NHS advises washing for about 20 seconds.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking germs can be seen, so hands that look clean must be clean; thinking a quick rinse with water is enough; skipping thumbs, nails and the backs of the hands; and sneezing into hands, which then spread germs to everything touched. It also helps to say that not all germs are bad, so children do not become fearful.","In the lab your child touches four things with a germy hand and sees the germs spread. They try water only and then soap and scrubbing, and scrub every part of the hands for 20 seconds. Then they sort moments into wash your hands or no need, and try three ways to sneeze to see which one catches the germs."]},"familyTip":"Rub a little glitter or flour into your child's hands to stand in for germs. Ask them to touch a cup and a toy, then look at what is left behind. Then try rinsing with water only, and then with soap, scrubbing every part while you both sing Happy Birthday twice. Which got more off?","keyIdea":"Germs are tiny living things, far too small to see, and some of them can make us ill. They spread from our hands to everything we touch, and from sneezes and coughs. Washing with soap and water, scrubbing every part of the hands for about 20 seconds, gets germs off much better than water alone. Good times to wash are before eating and after the toilet, touching animals or playing outside. Catch sneezes in a tissue, bin it, then wash your hands.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Wash your hands, or no need? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Wash hands","No need"],"items":[["before lunch",0],["listening to a story",1],["after the toilet",0],["looking at the sky",1],["after stroking a cat",0],["singing a song",1],["after blowing your nose",0],["drawing a picture",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsoap","caption":"Flagship lab: spread germs by touch, compare water and soap, scrub every part, sort wash times and catch a sneeze.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_lp1eTsQen7":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your heart is a…","choices":["muscle","bone","bag of air"],"answer":0,"hint":"It squeezes, like the muscles in your arm.","explain":"The heart is a muscle. Bones hold you up, and air goes into your lungs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You are fast asleep. What does your heart do?","choices":["keeps beating, slowly","stops till morning","beats as fast as it can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does your body still need blood at night?","explain":"The heart never stops. In sleep it beats more slowly, but it keeps pumping."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Does blood go to your toes?","choices":["yes, to every part of you","no, only to your chest","only when you run"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the tubes going down your legs.","explain":"Blood vessels reach every part of the body, toes too, all the time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How big is your heart?","choices":["about as big as your fist","as big as your head","as small as a pea"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make a fist and look at it.","explain":"Your heart is about the size of your own fist, and it grows as you grow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where is your heart?","choices":["in your chest","in your tummy","in your head"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put your hand where you feel the thump.","explain":"The heart is in your chest, a little to the left, behind your ribs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"After the blood goes out to your arm, what happens?","choices":["it comes back to the heart","it stays in the arm","it turns into air"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the tubes going out and the tubes coming back.","explain":"Blood goes out in some blood vessels and comes back to the heart in others, round and round."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Ava feels {a} beats. Then she feels {b} more beats. How many beats in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at the first number of beats and count on the extra ones.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} beats in all.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one makes your heart beat fastest?","choices":["running a race","sitting on a chair","sleeping in bed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one makes you puff and pant?","explain":"Running makes your muscles work hard, so the heart pumps faster. Sleeping is the slowest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Leo's heart beats {a} times in 10 seconds while he sleeps and {b} times while he runs. How many more when he runs?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Take the sleeping beats away from the running beats.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a}. His heart beats {=b-a} more times while he runs, because his muscles need more blood.","params":{"a":[11,14],"b":[24,30]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does your heart do?","choices":["pumps blood round your body","helps you think","breaks up your food"],"answer":0,"hint":"It squeezes again and again, like a pump.","explain":"The heart is a muscle that pumps blood. Your brain does the thinking and your tummy breaks up food."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Blood moves through tubes. What are they called?","choices":["blood vessels","bones","muscles"],"answer":0,"hint":"The tubes carry blood, so the name starts with blood.","explain":"Blood vessels are the tubes that carry blood to every part of the body and back to the heart."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Where can you feel your heartbeat?","choices":["on your wrist, below your thumb","on your elbow","on your knee"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try two fingers on the inside of your wrist.","explain":"At the wrist a blood vessel is close to the skin, so you can feel each beat. Your chest works too."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam counts {a} beats in 10 seconds. After running he counts {b} beats. How many more beats after running?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Take the smaller number away from the bigger one.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a}. That is {=b-a} more beats, because his heart beats faster after running.","params":{"a":[12,16],"b":[22,28]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p6","prompt":"The heart squeezes {a} times, then {b} more times. How many squeezes is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two numbers of squeezes together.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} squeezes. Each squeeze is one heartbeat.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Your heart is in your chest. It is a strong muscle, about as big as your fist.","The heart is a pump. It squeezes, then rests, then squeezes again. Each squeeze pushes blood out.","Blood moves in tubes called blood vessels. They go to every part of you: your head, arms, legs and toes. Then the blood comes back to the heart.","Put two fingers on your wrist, below your thumb. Can you feel a tap, tap, tap? That is your heartbeat. Run on the spot, then feel again. It is faster now!"],"adult":["In England, Year 2 science asks children to describe the importance of exercise for humans, and Year 6 names the heart, blood vessels and blood as the circulatory system. This lesson gives the younger child the core picture: the heart is a muscle, it pumps blood through blood vessels to every part of the body, and you can feel it working.","Words to use: heart, muscle, pump, blood, blood vessels (tubes), heartbeat, pulse. Arteries carry blood away from the heart and veins bring it back; at this age 'out in one tube, back in another' is enough. A child's heart at rest beats roughly 70 to 120 times a minute, more after exercise and fewer in sleep.","Common mistakes to listen for: thinking the heart stops or 'rests' at night; thinking blood only goes to the chest or the top half of the body; thinking the heart is shaped like a Valentine's heart or sits in the middle of the tummy; and thinking blood in veins is blue. Blood is always red. Veins only look blue through skin.","In the lab your child squeezes the heart, joins tubes from the heart to the head, arms, legs and tummy, finds the beat at the chest and wrist, compares sitting with running, and checks that the heart still beats during sleep."]},"familyTip":"Find your pulses together: two fingers on the wrist below the thumb, or a hand flat on the left side of the chest. Count the beats while a sand timer or phone counts 10 seconds. Then do 20 star jumps and count again. Which number is bigger? Make a fist to show how big the heart is.","keyIdea":"Your heart is a muscle in your chest that pumps blood all the time. Each squeeze pushes blood out through tubes called blood vessels to every part of your body, from your head to your toes, and the blood comes back to the heart in other blood vessels. You can feel each squeeze as a heartbeat: put a hand on your chest or two fingers on your wrist. The heart beats faster when you run and slower when you sleep, but it never stops.","visual":{"type":"bodylab","caption":"Explore the circulatory system: find the heart and the blood vessels that carry blood around the body.","opts":{"system":"circulatory"}},"lab":{"type":"flagheart","caption":"Flagship lab: squeeze the heart, join blood vessels to each body part, feel the beat at chest and wrist, and compare sleep, sitting and running.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_tiPyEkm4cU":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The tube to the lungs is the…","choices":["windpipe","ear canal","ankle bone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Name the air tube in the throat.","explain":"The windpipe carries air from the mouth or nose to the lungs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"On breathe-in the chest…","choices":["rises","falls and stays empty","locks shut"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens as lungs fill with air?","explain":"Breathe-in fills the lungs so the chest rises."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Oxygen is…","choices":["needed by the body","always the waste gas","never in air"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which gas from air does the body need to stay alive?","explain":"Oxygen is needed by the body. Carbon dioxide is the waste out-breath."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Carbon dioxide here is…","choices":["waste we breathe out","the only gas lungs keep","a type of food"],"answer":0,"hint":"What leaves on the out-breath?","explain":"Carbon dioxide is waste gas we breathe out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A breath story has {n} parts. You already ticked {k}. How many left?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract ticked parts from the full story count.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} story parts still to tick.","params":{"n":[3,5],"k":[0,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} children each take {k} slow breaths. How many breaths in total?","answer":"n*k","hint":"Multiply children by breaths each.","explain":"{n} × {k} = {=n*k} breaths in total.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Air stops at the mouth only?","choices":["No — it goes to the lungs","Yes — lungs are empty forever","Yes — windpipes are closed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Follow the path past the mouth.","explain":"Air continues down the windpipe into the lungs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which list is the air path in order?","choices":["nose/mouth, windpipe, lungs","lungs, ankles, ears","food tube only, then toes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start where air enters, then the tube, then the lungs.","explain":"Air path order: nose or mouth, windpipe, lungs."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Air enters through the…","choices":["nose or mouth","ankles","fingertips"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where you smell and taste air.","explain":"Air goes in through the nose or mouth, not the ankles or fingertips."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Lungs take…","choices":["oxygen","only carbon dioxide","gravel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which gas does the body need from air?","explain":"Lungs take oxygen. Carbon dioxide is what we breathe out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A class lists {n} true breath facts and {k} mistakes. How many true facts?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the true breath facts.","explain":"They listed {n} true facts. The {k} mistakes are separate.","params":{"n":[2,4],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A breath path has {n} stops. You already marked {k}. How many stops left?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract marked stops from the full path count.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} path stops still to mark.","params":{"n":[3,5],"k":[0,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"We breathe out…","choices":["carbon dioxide waste","only fresh oxygen","liquid metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"What waste gas leaves the lungs?","explain":"We breathe out carbon dioxide waste, not a store of fresh oxygen."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you breathe in, air goes through your nose or mouth, down your windpipe, into your lungs.","Your lungs take oxygen from the air. Your body needs oxygen to stay alive.","When you breathe out, you send out carbon dioxide. That is waste gas.","Feel your chest: it rises when you breathe in and falls when you breathe out."],"adult":["Ages 5–7 human body: nose/mouth → windpipe → lungs; oxygen in, carbon dioxide out; chest movement. KS1 animals including humans — basic needs include air. Words: windpipe, lungs, oxygen, carbon dioxide.","Common mistakes: thinking we breathe out oxygen; thinking air stops at the mouth; mixing the windpipe with the food tube; ignoring chest movement.","In the lab your child builds the air path, chooses oxygen in and carbon dioxide out, moves the chest in and out, matches gas jobs, and ticks the full breath story."]},"familyTip":"Put a hand on the ribs and take three slow breaths together. Ask 'what is going in?' and 'what is going out?'","keyIdea":"Air goes in through the nose or mouth, down the windpipe, into the lungs. Lungs take oxygen the body needs. We breathe out carbon dioxide waste. The chest rises on breathe-in and falls on breathe-out.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"In-breath or out-breath? Drag each idea, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Breathe in","Breathe out"],"items":[["oxygen into the body",0],["carbon dioxide waste leaves",1],["chest rises",0],["chest falls",1],["air down the windpipe",0],["waste gas leaves the lungs",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbreath","caption":"Flagship lab: build the air path, choose oxygen in and carbon dioxide out, move the chest, match gas jobs, and complete the breath story.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_SdIWVjzopp":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What do we call all your bones joined together?","choices":["Your skeleton","Your muscles","Your skin"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the word for the whole frame of bones inside you.","explain":"All your bones joined together make your skeleton."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which bones keep your heart and lungs safe?","choices":["The ribcage","The skull","The leg bones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your heart and lungs are in your chest. Feel the bumpy bones there.","explain":"Your ribs make a cage round your heart and lungs, so we call it the ribcage."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Can a muscle push a bone?","choices":["No, it can only pull","Yes, always","Only when you run"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the arm in the lab. Which muscle made it straight again?","explain":"Muscles only pull. A different muscle pulls the other way to make your arm straight."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Is your heart a bone or a muscle?","choices":["A muscle","A bone","Neither, it is a rib"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your heart squeezes to pump blood. Can a hard bone squeeze?","explain":"Your heart is a muscle. It squeezes all day and night. The ribs round it are bones."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Your ribs come in pairs, one on each side. A model shows {n} pairs. How many ribs is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"A pair is 2. Count 2 for each pair.","explain":"{n} pairs of ribs is {=2*n} ribs. Most people have 12 pairs, which is 24 ribs.","params":{"n":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Where is your spine?","choices":["Down the middle of your back","Inside your head","In your arm"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is also called the backbone.","explain":"The spine, or backbone, runs down the middle of your back and helps you stand tall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What happens to the front muscle in your arm when you bend it?","choices":["It gets short and fat","It gets long and thin","It turns hard like a bone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feel the front of your arm as you bend it.","explain":"The muscle gets short and fat as it pulls. It feels hard, but it is still a soft muscle, not a bone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What are muscles joined to?","choices":["Bones","Nothing","Only the skin"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do muscles pull on to move you?","explain":"Muscles are joined to bones. When they pull, the bones move."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Your skeleton puzzle has a skull, a ribcage and a spine. You add {n} more bones. How many bones are in your puzzle now?","answer":"3+n","hint":"Count the skull, the ribcage and the spine first. That is 3. Then add the new bones.","explain":"3 bones and {n} more make {=3+n} bones. Every bone you add is part of the skeleton.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What would your body be like with no bones?","choices":["Floppy, like jelly","Just the same","Taller"],"answer":0,"hint":"What holds you up and gives you your shape?","explain":"Bones hold you up. With no bones, your body could not keep its shape and would flop down."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You bend your arm {n} times and make it straight {n} times. Each move is one muscle pull. How many pulls is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Bending is one pull. Making it straight is another pull, by a different muscle.","explain":"{n} bends and {n} straightens make {=2*n} pulls. Every move is a muscle pulling, never pushing.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which bone keeps your brain safe?","choices":["The skull","The ribcage","The spine"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your brain is in your head. Which bone is like a helmet?","explain":"The skull is the hard bone round your brain, like a helmet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A skeleton puzzle has {n} bones. You have put {k} in place. How many bones are left to put in?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the bones you have placed away from all the bones.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} bones to put in. All the bones together make the skeleton.","params":{"n":[8,12],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How does a muscle move a bone?","choices":["It gets short and pulls the bone","It pushes the bone away","It turns into a bone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bend your arm. Does the front of it get short and fat, or long and thin?","explain":"A muscle gets short and fat and pulls the bone. Muscles can only pull."}],"explain":{"kid":["Inside you there are lots of bones. All your bones joined together make your skeleton. Bones are hard and strong. They hold you up and give you your shape. With no bones, you would be floppy, like jelly.","Some bones keep soft parts safe. Your skull is like a hard helmet. It keeps your brain safe. Your ribs make a cage round your heart and lungs. Your spine, or backbone, runs down your back and helps you stand up tall.","Muscles are soft and stretchy. They are joined to your bones. To move a bone, a muscle gets short and fat and pulls it. Bend your arm and feel the front of it go hard. That is a muscle pulling.","A muscle can only pull. It can not push. So to make your arm straight again, a muscle at the back of your arm pulls the other way. Your heart is a muscle too. It squeezes all day and all night."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. This builds the National Curriculum in England Year 1 aim to identify, name, draw and label the basic parts of the human body, and leads into Year 3 animals including humans: humans have skeletons and muscles for support, protection and movement. NGSS 4-LS1-1 later asks how internal structures support survival. Key words: bone, skeleton, skull, ribcage, ribs, spine (backbone), joint, muscle, pull, support, protect.","An adult skeleton has about 206 bones; a newborn has around 270 to 300 that later fuse. The skull protects the brain, the ribcage protects the heart and lungs, and the spine supports the body and protects the spinal cord. Skeletal muscles are joined to bones by tendons and move them only by contracting (pulling). They work in pairs: the biceps bends the elbow and the triceps straightens it. The heart is a different kind of muscle, called cardiac muscle.","Common mistakes: thinking bones are not alive or do nothing but sit there; thinking muscles push as well as pull; thinking the skeleton is one big bone or only the bones you can feel; putting the brain under the ribs or the heart in the skull; and calling the heart a bone because it sits inside the ribcage. The lab, the practice and the challenge target each of these.","In the lab your child takes the bones out of a body and watches it flop, then puts them back; drags the skull, ribcage, spine, arm bones and leg bones into place; gives the brain, heart and lungs the bone that keeps each one safe; drags a hand to bend an arm and sees the front muscle shorten and bulge, then the back muscle pull it straight; and sorts six cards into bone or muscle."]},"familyTip":"Go on a bone hunt together: gently feel your skull, the bumps of your spine, your ribs and the long bone in your leg, and say what each one does. Then hold your child's upper arm while they bend and straighten it, so they feel the front muscle get short and fat as it pulls.","keyIdea":"Inside your body is a skeleton: all your bones joined together. Bones are hard and strong. They hold you up and give your body its shape. Some bones keep soft parts safe: the skull covers the brain, and the ribcage covers the heart and lungs. Muscles are joined to bones. A muscle moves a bone by getting shorter and pulling it. Muscles can only pull, never push, so another muscle pulls the bone back the other way.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a bone or a muscle? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Bone","Muscle"],"items":[["Skull",0],["Heart",1],["Ribcage",0],["Biceps (front of the upper arm)",1],["Spine",0],["Muscles in your cheeks",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbones","caption":"Flagship lab: take the bones out and watch the body flop, build a skeleton, guard the brain, heart and lungs, bend an arm, sort bone or muscle.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_pJ5zsocdNx":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the boss of your body?","choices":["Your brain","Your heart","Your tummy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part thinks and decides?","explain":"The brain is the boss. The heart pumps blood and the tummy breaks up food."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What carries messages from your brain to your muscles?","choices":["Nerves","Bones","Blood"],"answer":0,"hint":"They are like thin wires all through your body.","explain":"Nerves carry messages. Blood carries food and air, and bones hold you up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A dog barks {n} times. Each bark sends 1 message from your ears to your brain. How many messages?","answer":"n","hint":"One bark makes one message, so count the barks.","explain":"{n} barks make {n} messages from your ears to your brain.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia says her legs decide to walk all by themselves. What is right?","choices":["Her brain tells her legs to walk","Mia is right","Her eyes tell her legs to walk"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which part of the body is the boss.","explain":"Legs do not decide. The brain sends a message along nerves to the leg muscles."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What does your brain do when you are asleep?","choices":["It keeps you breathing","It switches off","It stops all night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do you stop breathing when you sleep?","explain":"Your brain never switches off. Asleep, it keeps you breathing and warm, and it makes dreams."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You blink {a} times, then {b} more times. Each blink needs a message from the brain. How many messages?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the first blinks and the next blinks.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} messages to your eye muscles.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A lemon tastes sour. Which sense tells your brain?","choices":["Your tongue","Your eyes","Your ears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part do you taste with?","explain":"Your tongue tastes. It sends the message to your brain along nerves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What keeps your brain safe?","choices":["Your hard skull","Your soft hair","Your tummy"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is hard, like a helmet.","explain":"The skull is hard bone around the brain. Hair is soft and only keeps you warm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which order is right?","choices":["Sense notices, brain decides, muscles move","Muscles move, brain decides, sense notices","Brain decides, muscles move, sense notices"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has to happen before the brain can decide?","explain":"A sense notices first and tells the brain. The brain decides. Then nerves tell muscles to move."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where is your brain?","choices":["Inside your head","Inside your tummy","Inside your foot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the part with a hard skull, like a helmet.","explain":"Your brain is inside your head. The hard skull keeps it safe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You have 5 senses. You have used {n} of them today. How many more can you try?","answer":"5-n","hint":"Start at 5 and take away the senses you used.","explain":"5 − {n} = {=5-n}. Eyes, ears, nose, tongue and skin make 5 senses.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You smell a cake. Which sense sends the message to your brain?","choices":["Your nose","Your ears","Your hands"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of your face do you sniff with?","explain":"Your nose smells. It sends a message along nerves to your brain."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam's brain sends {a} messages to wave and then {b} messages to kick. How many messages is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the waving messages and the kicking messages.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} messages. Each one zooms along nerves to the muscles.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Your hand catches a ball. What happened first?","choices":["Your eyes saw the ball","Your hand moved","Your legs walked"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sense notices first, then the brain decides, then muscles move.","explain":"Eyes see the ball first. Then the brain decides to catch, and nerves tell the hand to move."}],"explain":{"kid":["Your brain is the boss of your body. It is inside your head. A hard skull keeps it safe, like a helmet. Your brain thinks, feels and remembers. It also tells every part of you what to do.","Your senses help the brain. Eyes see. Ears hear. Your nose smells. Your tongue tastes. Your skin feels. Each sense sends a message to the brain along thin wires called nerves.","The brain gets the message and decides what to do. Then it sends a message down the nerves to your muscles. The muscles move. Eyes see a ball. The brain says catch it. Your hands move and catch.","Messages on nerves are very fast. Some go faster than a car on a big road. Your brain works all the time, even at night. While you sleep, it keeps you breathing and warm, and it makes dreams."],"adult":["Ages 5 to 7. This topic sits in the animals including humans strand of the National Curriculum in England (Year 1: identify the parts of the body and say which part goes with each sense) and links to NGSS 1-LS1-1 and 4-LS1-2, where animals take in information through their senses, process it in the brain and respond. The model taught is the simple loop used in KS1 and early KS2 resources: senses (receptors) send messages along sensory nerves to the brain, the brain decides, and motor nerves carry messages to muscles (effectors).","The lab has five tasks. Learners tap a body to find the boss, then send five sense messages up the nerves to the brain. They put two short stories into sense, brain, muscles order. They drag a message from the brain down the nerves to the leg, arm or face to do a job. Last, they pick what the brain still does at night: breathing, keeping warm and dreaming. Fast nerve messages travel at up to about 100 metres per second, which is faster than a car on a motorway.","Common mistakes. Young children often say the heart does the thinking, or that hands and feet move by themselves. Some think messages travel in the blood, or that the brain switches off at night. Take care with reflexes: pulling a hand away from something hot is a fast reflex through the spinal cord, so this lesson uses caught balls and called names as its brain examples, not hot objects."]},"familyTip":"Play catch with a soft ball. Each time, say the three steps out loud: my eyes see it, my brain decides, my hands catch. Then try with eyes shut and notice how much harder it is when your eyes cannot send the message.","keyIdea":"Your brain is the boss of your body. It sits inside your head, safe under a hard skull. Your senses (eyes, ears, nose, tongue and skin) notice things and send messages along thin nerves to your brain. The brain thinks and decides what to do. Then it sends messages along nerves to your muscles, and they move. Eyes see a ball, the brain decides to catch it, and nerves tell your hands to move. Your brain never switches off: even when you sleep, it keeps you breathing and warm.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this part notice things or move you? Drag each card, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Notices things (sense)","Moves you (muscles)"],"items":[["Eyes",0],["Leg muscles",1],["Ears",0],["Arm muscles",1],["Nose",0],["Face muscles for a smile",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnerve","caption":"Flagship lab: find the boss, send sense messages up nerves, order sense, brain and muscles, and see the brain at night.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
